('area:paradiski','routes','line',11,'Plagne Centre','{}'::text[],'review','loader','routes.rows.3.to'),
('area:paradiski','routes','line',12,'Green and blue','{}'::text[],'review','loader','routes.rows.3.grade'),
('area:paradiski','routes','note',3,'The way back takes one lift ride fewer than the way out.','{}'::text[],'review','loader','routes.rows.3.note'),
('area:paradiski','routes','line',13,'Montalbert','{}'::text[],'review','loader','routes.rows.4.from'),
('area:paradiski','routes','line',14,'Villaroger','{}'::text[],'review','loader','routes.rows.4.to'),
('area:paradiski','routes','line',15,'Green and blue','{}'::text[],'review','loader','routes.rows.4.grade'),
('area:paradiski','routes','note',4,'The two ends of the area: out by the Montalbert gondola and the Vanoise Express, back by the Comborcière chair and the same cable car.','{}'::text[],'review','loader','routes.rows.4.note'),
('area:paradiski','routes','note',5,'Every one of these days works on green and blue runs alone. Only from Champagny-en-Vanoise does a black run save a lift ride, and only on the way to the Les Arcs side.','{}'::text[],'review','loader','routes.after'),
('area:paradiski','areas','heading',0,'The ten sectors compared','{}'::text[],'review','loader','areas.h2'),
('area:paradiski','areas','line',0,'The pass covers villages built for skiing high on the mountain, such as Belle Plagne and Arc 2000, and older villages lower down, such as Montchavin, Champagny, Peisey and Villaroger, whose lowest lifts start between {{f:F000348|n}} and {{f:F000316}}. Where a sector sits relative to the Vanoise Express decides how quickly the other resort can be reached.',array['F000316','F000348']::text[],'review','loader','areas.intro'),
('area:paradiski','areas','caption',0,'Sectors on the Paradiski pass','{}'::text[],'review','loader','areas.caption'),
('area:paradiski','areas','line',1,'Linked to Plagne Centre by one gondola','{}'::text[],'review','loader','areas.rows.0.net'),
('area:paradiski','areas','note',0,'The highest share of drag lifts in the area.','{}'::text[],'review','loader','areas.rows.0.note'),
('area:paradiski','areas','line',2,'Linked, the hub of La Plagne','{}'::text[],'review','loader','areas.rows.1.net'),
('area:paradiski','areas','note',1,'More lifts than any other sector, and nearly half its main lifts are drags.','{}'::text[],'review','loader','areas.rows.1.note'),
('area:paradiski','areas','line',3,'Linked, below the Roche de Mio and the glacier','{}'::text[],'review','loader','areas.rows.2.net'),
('area:paradiski','areas','note',2,'The highest share of green and blue in the area, and the Glaciers gondola.','{}'::text[],'review','loader','areas.rows.2.note'),
('area:paradiski','areas','line',4,'Linked over the ridge from Plagne Centre','{}'::text[],'review','loader','areas.rows.3.net'),
('area:paradiski','areas','note',3,'The lowest share of green and blue in the area.','{}'::text[],'review','loader','areas.rows.3.note'),
('area:paradiski','areas','line',5,'Linked; La Plagne end of the Vanoise Express','{}'::text[],'review','loader','areas.rows.4.net'),
('area:paradiski','areas','note',4,'The lowest lift base in La Plagne.','{}'::text[],'review','loader','areas.rows.4.note'),
('area:paradiski','areas','line',6,'Linked; Les Arcs end of the Vanoise Express','{}'::text[],'review','loader','areas.rows.5.net'),
('area:paradiski','areas','note',5,'A gondola with {{f:F000253|w}} seats a cabin replaces the Lonzagne lift from Peisey village this Christmas.',array['F000253']::text[],'review','loader','areas.rows.5.note'),
('area:paradiski','areas','line',7,'Linked; top of the funicular from Bourg-Saint-Maurice','{}'::text[],'review','loader','areas.rows.6.net'),
('area:paradiski','areas','note',6,'The first of the Arc villages, opened in {{f:F000241}}, with the SPARK snowpark.',array['F000241']::text[],'review','loader','areas.rows.6.note'),
('area:paradiski','areas','line',8,'Linked, the centre of Les Arcs','{}'::text[],'review','loader','areas.rows.7.net'),
('area:paradiski','areas','note',7,'The fewest drag lifts of any sector.','{}'::text[],'review','loader','areas.rows.7.note'),
('area:paradiski','areas','line',9,'Linked, the highest village of Les Arcs','{}'::text[],'review','loader','areas.rows.8.net'),
('area:paradiski','areas','note',8,'The Aiguille Rouge cable car reaches the highest lift top in the area.','{}'::text[],'review','loader','areas.rows.8.note'),
('area:paradiski','areas','line',10,'Linked, below the Aiguille Rouge','{}'::text[],'review','loader','areas.rows.9.net'),
('area:paradiski','areas','note',9,'The lowest lift base in the area, at the foot of the long run from the Aiguille Rouge.','{}'::text[],'review','loader','areas.rows.9.note'),
('area:paradiski','areas','note',10,'From the OpenStreetMap lift and piste data (snapshot 24 October 2026) with heights from the Copernicus 90 m elevation model. Green and blue is the share of mapped piste length; drag lifts include platters and T bars as a share of the main lifts. Mapped piste is incomplete, so the shares are useful for comparing sectors but not as totals, and they are being checked against the official 2026/27 piste map.','{}'::text[],'review','loader','areas.note'),
('area:paradiski','terrain','heading',0,'Blues across both resorts','{}'::text[],'review','loader','terrain.h2'),
('area:paradiski','terrain','para',0,'Les Arcs and Peisey-Vallandry count {{f:F000235}} green, {{f:F000236}} blue, {{f:F000237}} red and {{f:F000238}} black runs, and La Plagne counts {{f:F000229}} runs, {{f:F000230}} of them blue. skiresort.com measures {{f:F000075}} of the piste across Paradiski as easy, {{f:F000076}} as intermediate and {{f:F000077}} as difficult.',array['F000075','F000076','F000077','F000229','F000230','F000235','F000236','F000237','F000238']::text[],'review','loader','terrain.paras.0'),
('area:paradiski','terrain','para',1,'In the map data every sector can be reached from every other on green and blue alone, and allowing reds never shortens a journey. The one shortcut is a black run from Champagny-en-Vanoise, which saves a lift ride on the way to Montchavin-Les Coches and everything beyond it: on green and blue, Arc 2000 takes {{m:easy.champagny.arc-2000|w}} lift rides from Champagny instead of {{m:all.champagny.arc-2000|w}}.','{}'::text[],'review','loader','terrain.paras.1'),
('area:paradiski','terrain','para',2,'That makes the area easy to share across a group of mixed ability, on one condition: the meeting point has to be on the right side of the cable car. From Plagne Bellecôte, Arc 1800 is {{m:all.bellecote.arc-1800|w}} lift rides away; from Montalbert it is {{m:all.montalbert.arc-1800|w}}.','{}'::text[],'review','loader','terrain.paras.2'),
('area:paradiski','terrain','para',3,'The steepest and longest skiing is high in Les Arcs. The run from the Aiguille Rouge to Villaroger is {{f:F000254}} long and drops more than {{f:F000255}}, and Arc 2000 has the speed skiing track used at the {{f:F000245}} Olympics. In La Plagne the Glaciers gondola climbs to {{f:F000232}} by the operator''s figure, the highest point of the resort.',array['F000232','F000245','F000254','F000255']::text[],'review','loader','terrain.paras.3'),
('area:paradiski','terrain','heading',1,'Lift rides between the areas, and the grade they need','{}'::text[],'review','loader','terrain.matrixTitle'),
('area:paradiski','terrain','note',0,'Rows are where the day starts, columns where it goes. Every cell can be reached on green and blue runs and shows the fewest lift rides on those runs. From the OpenStreetMap piste grades, snapshot 24 October 2026; untagged or wrongly tagged runs can distort the result, and each one is being checked against the official map.','{}'::text[],'review','loader','terrain.matrixNote'),
('area:paradiski','snow','heading',0,'Height decides the snow','{}'::text[],'review','loader','snow.h2'),
('area:paradiski','snow','para',0,'Most of the skiing sits well above the villages. The lowest lift in Arc 2000 starts at {{f:F000340}}, and on the elevation model the Aiguille Rouge cable car climbs to {{f:F000341}} and the Glaciers gondola to {{f:F000293}}. The edges are low: Villaroger, Montchavin, Champagny and Peisey all have lifts starting between {{f:F000348|n}} and {{f:F000316}}.',array['F000293','F000316','F000340','F000341','F000348']::text[],'review','loader','snow.paras.0'),
('area:paradiski','snow','para',1,'The weather model behind the snowfall figures covers the whole area with one grid cell, so it cannot tell La Plagne from Les Arcs, but it shows plainly what height does. In a typical season between 1991 and 2020, about {{f:F000361}} of snow fell at 1,600 m, {{f:F000363}} at 2,100 m and {{f:F000365}} at 2,600 m.',array['F000361','F000363','F000365']::text[],'review','loader','snow.paras.1'),
('area:paradiski','snow','para',2,'The lower slopes have lost the most. At 1,600 m the typical season fell from {{f:F000362}} in 1961 to 1990 to {{f:F000361}} in 1991 to 2020, and every one of the last five seasons sat in the lowest third of the 76 year record. At 2,600 m the fall was smaller, from {{f:F000366}} to {{f:F000365}}, and two of the last five seasons were that poor.',array['F000361','F000362','F000365','F000366']::text[],'review','loader','snow.paras.2'),
('area:paradiski','snow','para',3,'On this evidence the high villages of either resort are the safer base in a thin winter. The calendar points the same way: the Vanoise Express and the lower sectors of La Plagne, Montalbert, Champagny and Montchavin-Les Coches, close on {{f:F000224|dm}}, a week before the high ground of both resorts on {{f:F000226|dm}}. The model gives snowfall rather than depth on the ground, and measured depths from weather stations are still being collected.',array['F000224','F000226']::text[],'review','loader','snow.paras.3'),
('area:paradiski','snow','heading',1,'Lift heights by sector','{}'::text[],'review','loader','snow.heightsTitle'),
('area:paradiski','snow','caption',0,'Lowest lift base to highest lift top in each sector. From OpenStreetMap lift positions and the Copernicus 90 m elevation model, which reads a little low on the highest summits.','{}'::text[],'review','loader','snow.heightsCaption'),
('area:paradiski','snow','heading',2,'Seasonal snowfall at three heights, 1950 to 2026','{}'::text[],'review','loader','snow.chartTitle'),
('area:paradiski','snow','caption',1,'Modelled snowfall from November to April, from the ERA5 reanalysis via the Open-Meteo historical weather API, for the grid cell at 45.5 N, 6.75 E adjusted to each height. Dots are single seasons; lines are 11 season running means. These are model values, not measurements on a piste.','{}'::text[],'review','loader','snow.chartCaption'),
('area:paradiski','snow','line',0,'Modelled snowfall from November to April. Dots are single seasons; lines are 11 season running means. Median 1991 to 2020: {{f:F000361}} at 1,600 m, {{f:F000363}} at 2,100 m, {{f:F000365}} at 2,600 m.',array['F000361','F000363','F000365']::text[],'review','loader','snow.chartDesc'),
('area:paradiski','snow','line',1,'<b>No conditions are reported here yet.</b> Both resorts are due to open on {{f:F000225}}. Until live data arrives, the official sources below give open lifts, pistes and forecasts.',array['F000225']::text[],'review','loader','snow.conditions.status'),
('area:paradiski','snow','line',2,'Lifts and pistes open','{}'::text[],'review','loader','snow.conditions.items.0.dt'),
('area:paradiski','snow','line',3,'<a href="https://en.la-plagne.com/opening-dates">La Plagne</a> and <a href="https://www.lesarcs.com/en">Les Arcs</a>','{}'::text[],'review','loader','snow.conditions.items.0.dd'),
('area:paradiski','snow','line',4,'Mountain forecast','{}'::text[],'review','loader','snow.conditions.items.1.dt'),
('area:paradiski','snow','line',5,'<a href="https://meteofrance.com/meteo-montagne">Météo-France mountain weather</a>','{}'::text[],'review','loader','snow.conditions.items.1.dd'),
('area:paradiski','snow','line',6,'Avalanche danger','{}'::text[],'review','loader','snow.conditions.items.2.dt'),
('area:paradiski','snow','line',7,'<a href="https://meteofrance.com/meteo-montagne">Météo-France bulletins</a> for the Savoie massifs, published daily in winter','{}'::text[],'review','loader','snow.conditions.items.2.dd'),
('area:paradiski','snow','line',8,'Terrain beyond the marked pistes is not controlled. The avalanche bulletin and local advice take precedence over anything on this page.','{}'::text[],'review','loader','snow.conditions.safety'),
('area:paradiski','pass','heading',0,'Does the Paradiski pass pay?','{}'::text[],'review','loader','pass.h2'),
('area:paradiski','pass','para',0,'A six day adult Paradiski pass costs {{f:F000066}} in high season. The single resort passes undercut it by very little: {{f:F000258}} for La Plagne and {{f:F000265}} for Les Arcs and Peisey-Vallandry, savings of {{diff:F000066,F000258}} and {{diff:F000066,F000265}}. Either gap is smaller than a single day on the Paradiski pass at {{f:F000065}}.',array['F000065','F000066','F000258','F000265']::text[],'review','loader','pass.paras.0'),
('area:paradiski','pass','para',1,'For a week the arithmetic favours Paradiski, as long as the Vanoise Express is running. It stops on {{f:F000224|dm}}, and in the last week of the season the two resorts can only be reached from each other by road. Les Arcs also sells a Premium version of the Paradiski pass at {{f:F000269}} for six days, which adds unlimited sledging, a zip line ride and a swimming pool entry.',array['F000224','F000269']::text[],'review','loader','pass.paras.1'),
('area:paradiski','pass','caption',0,'Single resort pass against Paradiski pass, adult, 2026/27','{}'::text[],'review','loader','pass.table.caption'),
('area:paradiski','pass','line',0,'La Plagne, six days','{}'::text[],'review','loader','pass.table.rows.0.0'),
('area:paradiski','pass','line',1,'{{f:F000258}}',array['F000258']::text[],'review','loader','pass.table.rows.0.1'),
